```
Reason:
The original implementation stored the components of the GUID structure (`a`, `b`, and `c`) in little-endian format, while the component `d` was being treated as big-endian. This inconsistency in byte order led to incorrect comparisons and representations of GUIDs, violating the EFI specification.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
guid->a = cpu_to_le32(guid->a);
guid->b = cpu_to_le16(guid->b);
guid->c = cpu_to_le16(guid->c);
guid->d = cpu_to_be16(guid->d);
```
Reason:
These statements ensure that all components of the GUID structure are consistently converted to the correct byte order as specified by the EFI standard. Specifically, `a`, `b`, and `c` are converted to little-endian format, while `d` is converted to big-endian format, thus resolving the byte order inconsistency that caused the original bug.

